  "pages": 20,
  "question": "How can AI-assisted work on an open problem persist as a record that people and models can check, understand and extend?",
  "precis": "A problem-sized world keeps one open problem with its established results, failed routes, experiments, literature and exact open obligations: a short paper for an expert and a long record for whoever continues. Lean checks the formal proofs, Comparator checks that each selected proof establishes exactly its separately written statement, and a claim registry records the wording, evidence class and limits of every public claim. A public Lean repository maintains eight open Erdős problems this way, with a contribution cycle published as agent skills. The paper compares the design with Prove2Me and states what remains a mathematician's judgement.",
  "owns": "the research architecture: problem-sized worlds, the path from a proof to a public claim, and the contribution cycle",
